{
    "word": "quinine",
    "lang_code": "en",
    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "04eb45a4-b71e-3941-bc4a-484015579ee9",
                "word": "quinine",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "70213ec4-e238-445a-8eb2-021ef2177f4f",
                        "entry_id": "04eb45a4-b71e-3941-bc4a-484015579ee9",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "quinines"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "e13d2804-6be4-3b75-b5e0-0b37c6652606",
                        "entry_id": "04eb45a4-b71e-3941-bc4a-484015579ee9",
                        "glosses": [
                            "An alkaloid with the chemical formula C₂₀H₂₄N₂O₂, originally derived from cinchona bark (from plants of the genus Cinchona), used to treat malaria and as an ingredient of tonic water, which presents as a bitter colourless powder; also, a drug containing quinine or a chemical compound derived from it."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "92d2348e-44c8-4005-8eca-eaf866294fc7",
                                "sense_id": "e13d2804-6be4-3b75-b5e0-0b37c6652606",
                                "text": "The alkali of yellow bark may be distinguished from cinchonine by the name of quinine."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "37e51235-7289-4c42-bbe9-234a4a557f1b",
                                "sense_id": "e13d2804-6be4-3b75-b5e0-0b37c6652606",
                                "text": "The quinine, being more potent than cinchonine, is generally preferred."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "59ba39de-e4a7-4b7d-8ab0-fd7125c92bfb",
                                "sense_id": "e13d2804-6be4-3b75-b5e0-0b37c6652606",
                                "text": "In spite of quinine, the men sickened day by day. Many of them, fine, strong, active fellows, who had never known what a day's sickness meant, went down before the malarious mist that gathered in the jungles."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "3b9c2205-96af-4583-a1be-3e0870a02ab7",
                                "sense_id": "e13d2804-6be4-3b75-b5e0-0b37c6652606",
                                "text": "He hadn't the faintest idea what to do with a cold in the head, he just took quinine and continued to blow his nose."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "6c6bc2b0-40f3-4816-b8dd-d82ce34053c5",
                                "sense_id": "e13d2804-6be4-3b75-b5e0-0b37c6652606",
                                "text": "“Die? Yes, they’ll all die—all these men. No bandages, no salves, no quinine, no chloroform. Oh, God, for some morphia! Just a little morphia for the worst ones. Just a little chloroform. God damn the Yankees! God damn the Yankees!”"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "aafb3129-5fef-4562-bcbb-e0a6b1cf6e33",
                                "sense_id": "e13d2804-6be4-3b75-b5e0-0b37c6652606",
                                "text": "Quinine, taken in the evening, is considered by the Persians as an aphrodisiac aid."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "356e5b9f-dcb2-40ab-8afb-ed8fdaacf796",
                                "sense_id": "e13d2804-6be4-3b75-b5e0-0b37c6652606",
                                "text": "I propose that the availability of increased stores of quinine under British control had a similar facilitating effect on the British colonial expansion into Africa in the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b35d0ffb-5689-4c71-af7b-4ae68e7e7a2b",
                                "sense_id": "e13d2804-6be4-3b75-b5e0-0b37c6652606",
                                "text": "So far, the daily dose of quinine had been bitter and very unpalatable. […] To make the medicine go down more easily, colonialists occasionally mixed the powder with sugar, water and gin."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
